# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the C unit suite (`make test SANITIZE=address`).
#
# Deliberately *narrower* than tests/lsan.supp next door, and the difference is
# load-bearing. That file carries a module-wide `leak:libglib-2.0`, which
# matches any leak whose stack passes through GLib - and oans holds nearly
# everything in GLib containers (GPtrArray, GHashTable, GRegex, GString), so
# the pattern suppresses most of oans's own heap along with GLib's.
#
# Measured, on this suite: removing one `g_free()` from glob_set_free() leaks
# 769,200 bytes in 32,050 allocations, LeakSanitizer names glob_set_new() as
# the allocation site - and `leak:libglib-2.0` suppresses every one of them, so
# the run exits 0. A mutation sweep found fourteen deletions of a free() or an
# unref() surviving an ASAN build for exactly this reason.
#
# What is left here is what the suppression was written for: GLib caches idle
# worker threads from its thread pools rather than joining them at exit, so the
# per-thread bookkeeping and TLS can still be live when LSAN scans. Those three
# are named by function, so they cannot swallow anything else.
#
# The unit suite currently needs none of this - it is leak-clean with no
# suppressions at all - so these are insurance against a GLib version that
# caches more eagerly, not a known requirement.
leak:g_thread_proxy
leak:g_private_get
leak:g_thread_self
